d3d11, dxmt: Say in the log what each slow frame waited for.

The half-minute lines say how many frames were slow and what a frame waited for on average,
which hides the slow ones: a frame of 160 ms among 3600 of 8 ms moves no average. Each
frame that takes over 50 ms now gets a line of its own as it is noticed, the first 24 of a
half minute:

  DXGI: slow frame (frame_cause_test.exe, 15:06:22.5): 119.8 ms: 119.7 ms for an earlier
  frame, 0 waits for the GPU (0.0 ms), 0 query flushes, commit 0.0 ms, rest 0.0 ms; 1 new
  pipeline, 1 shader compiled; the frame 4 before: encoding 112.9 ms (112.8 ms of it for 1
  pipeline, 0.0 ms for a drawable), GPU 1.8 ms

The frame is the time from one Present to the next on the thread that presents. Of it, the
line has what that thread waited for: an earlier frame (the Present before waits until the
frame the queue's latency names is done on the GPU, and a swap chain with a latency of its
own until that one is encoded), the GPU (a staging texture mapped before its copy was done),
a free command chunk ("commit"), and how often a query was asked for before it had come. The
rest is the game's own time and DXMT's on that thread, the time between a query's flushes
among it. Two of these waits happen in the Present before, after the frame's time was taken,
and are carried to the frame they fall into. Then come the pipelines and shaders that were
made during the frame, and what the frame that was waited for cost: its time on the encoding
thread, how much of that was a draw's wait for its pipeline (counted for the frame that is
being encoded, where patch 0013 counted it for the process) and how much the wait for a
drawable, and the GPU's time for its command buffers (the finishing thread adds it before it
says that the frame is done, so it is final when it is read). A queue latency of 15 frames or
more would have that frame's place taken, and the line says so.

macos/tests/frame_cause_test.c does four things to a frame and says what it did; the lines
of the same run say the same:
  a sleep of 150.3 ms                           160.3 ms: rest 151.3 ms
  2 new shaders and their pipeline              4 frames on, 119.8 ms: 119.7 ms for an earlier
                                                frame, whose encoding took 112.9 ms, 112.8 ms
                                                of it for 1 pipeline
  a staging texture mapped, waited 95.6 ms      104.1 ms: 1 wait for the GPU (95.5 ms)
  281782 GetData calls in 90.5 ms               100.1 ms: 281782 query flushes, rest 91.1 ms

Costs, measured with that test at 120 frames a second, the DLLs before patch 0013 against
these (two runs of 20 s each way): 0.08 us more for a command buffer on the finishing thread,
with 51 command buffers a frame; no difference for a bind, with 2000 binds and draws a frame.
What the presenting thread does more for a frame (two counters read, a few values kept) is
below what such runs can tell apart, which is 2.4 us a frame.

LOCAL TO THIS PORT, NOT FOR DXMT. Applied by macos/build-dxmt.sh to
github.com/athei/dxmt at 01e59e9bde030f105937f249afaac92ada01b4d7, after 0001 to 0014.
Written with an AI assistant (Claude). DXMT does not accept contributions made or
co-authored by AI (its CONTRIBUTING.md), so this patch must not be sent to it as a pull
request.
